{"text":"Evidence supports testing the objective before replacing SASRec. [gSASRec’s 2024 summary](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) attributes much of SASRec’s disadvantage against BERT4Rec to negative-sampling-induced overconfidence; controlling sampling substantially closes the gap. It recommends gBCE with calibration t=0.75 and 128–256 negatives, subject to compute limits. For RecHarness, a useful controlled comparison is unchanged SASRec with baseline BCE, multi-negative gBCE, and uniform sampled-softmax cross-entropy. Training margins alone cannot select among them.

Sampling correction depends on the objective and actual proposal distribution. [Correcting the LogQ Correction (RecSys 2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.09331) identifies a flaw in conventional correction: the positive is always included, whereas negatives are sampled. Its refined formulation accounts for that distinction and improves over standard correction. In-batch negatives offer efficient candidate reuse but follow item popularity; uniform evaluation candidates do not make in-batch training uniform. A [2024 sequential-recommendation comparison](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.17276) finds substantial dataset-dependent accuracy and popularity tradeoffs across random, popularity, mixed, and adaptive sampling.

Aggressive hard mining risks penalizing unobserved positives. [UFNRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.04116), an earlier relevant method, uses teacher soft labels and consistency regularization for suspected false negatives. Practical experiment proposal: first establish uniform multi-negative performance, then test a small semi-hard mixture excluding known training positives; treat false-negative identification as uncertain. Also compare batch size against negative count at matched cost: a [2026 preprint](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.11061) favors larger batches under its fixed-memory assumptions. Select exclusively by the pinned four-category macro validation HR@10, with matched GPU budgets; none of these papers establishes gains on this exact benchmark."}
